          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Let \\\\( x \\\\) and \\\\( y \\\\) be positive integers. If for each positive integer \\\\( n \\\\) we have that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(n y)^{2}+1 \\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nprove that \\\\( x=1 \\\\).\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"bmo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Assume for contradiction that \\\\(x>1\\\\).\\n\\nFor every odd prime \\\\(p\\\\mid y\\\\) with \\\\(p\\\\nmid x\\\\), let \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(x)\\\\) denote the multiplicative order of \\\\(x\\\\pmod p\\\\). Let \\\\(L\\\\) be the least common multiple of these orders, taking \\\\(L=1\\\\) if there are no such primes.\\n\\nChoose an odd prime \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\nmid x^{2L}+1\\\\), and put \\\\(k=\\\\ell L\\\\) and \\\\(N=x^{2k}+1\\\\). Define\\n\\\\[\\ne=\\\\begin{cases}2,&2\\\\mid y\\\\text{ and }x\\\\text{ is odd},\\\\\\\\1,&\\\\text{otherwise},\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\qquad Q=\\\\frac{N}{e}.\\n\\\\]\\nWe claim that \\\\(\\\\gcd(Q,xy)=1\\\\). Certainly \\\\(\\\\gcd(N,x)=1\\\\). If an odd prime \\\\(p\\\\mid y\\\\) also divides \\\\(x\\\\), then \\\\(N\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). If \\\\(p\\\\mid y\\\\) but \\\\(p\\\\nmid x\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(x)\\\\mid L\\\\), so\\n\\\\[\\nx^{2k}=x^{2\\\\ell L}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p,\\n\\\\]\\nand hence \\\\(N\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod p\\\\). Thus no odd prime divisor of \\\\(y\\\\) divides \\\\(N\\\\). If \\\\(x\\\\) is odd, then \\\\(N\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod8\\\\), so dividing by \\\\(e\\\\) removes the only possible common factor \\\\(2\\\\). This proves the claim.\\n\\nMoreover,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\operatorname{ord}_Q(x)=4k.\\n\\\\]\\nIndeed, \\\\(x^{2k}\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod Q\\\\), so the order divides \\\\(4k\\\\) but is not a divisor of \\\\(2k\\\\). If it were a proper divisor of \\\\(4k\\\\), it would be less than \\\\(2k\\\\); say it were \\\\(d<2k\\\\). Then \\\\(Q\\\\mid x^d-1\\\\), whereas\\n\\\\[\\n0<x^d-1\\\\le x^{2k-1}-1<\\\\frac{x^{2k}+1}{2}\\\\le Q,\\n\\\\]\\na contradiction.\\n\\nBy Fermat's congruence \\\\(x^\\\\ell\\\\equiv x\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nN=x^{2\\\\ell L}+1\\\\equiv x^{2L}+1\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod\\\\ell.\\n\\\\]\\nHence \\\\(\\\\gcd(\\\\ell,Q)=1\\\\).\\n\\nBecause \\\\(y\\\\) is invertible modulo \\\\(Q\\\\), choose \\\\(a\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nay\\\\equiv x^k\\\\pmod Q.\\n\\\\]\\nBy the Chinese remainder theorem, there is a residue class \\\\(c\\\\pmod{\\\\ell Q}\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nc\\\\equiv a\\\\pmod Q,\\\\qquad c\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod\\\\ell.\\n\\\\]\\nIt is coprime to \\\\(\\\\ell Q\\\\). By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, choose a prime \\\\(r\\\\equiv c\\\\pmod{\\\\ell Q}\\\\).\\n\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\nry\\\\equiv x^k\\\\pmod Q,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\((ry)^2\\\\equiv x^{2k}\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod Q\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nQ\\\\mid (ry)^2+1.\\n\\\\]\\nApplying the hypothesis with \\\\(n=r\\\\), and using \\\\(\\\\varphi(r)=r-1\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\nQ\\\\mid x^{r-1}-1.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_Q(x)=4k\\\\), we obtain \\\\(4k\\\\mid r-1\\\\), and in particular \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\mid r-1\\\\). But \\\\(r\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\), so \\\\(r-1\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\), a contradiction.\\n\\nThus \\\\(x>1\\\\) is impossible. Since \\\\(x\\\\) is a positive integer, necessarily \\\\(x=1\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
