tradiction.\\n\\nBy Fermat's congruence \\\\(x^\\\\ell\\\\equiv x\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nN=x^{2\\\\ell L}+1\\\\equiv x^{2L}+1\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod\\\\ell.\\n\\\\]\\nHence \\\\(\\\\gcd(\\\\ell,Q)=1\\\\).\\n\\nBecause \\\\(y\\\\) is invertible modulo \\\\(Q\\\\), choose \\\\(a\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nay\\\\equiv x^k\\\\pmod Q.\\n\\\\]\\nBy the Chinese remainder theorem, there is a residue class \\\\(c\\\\pmod{\\\\ell Q}\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nc\\\\equiv a\\\\pmod Q,\\\\qquad c\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod\\\\ell.\\n\\\\]\\nIt is coprime to \\\\(\\\\ell Q\\\\). By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, choose a prime \\\\(r\\\\equiv c\\\\pmod{\\\\ell Q}\\\\).\\n\\nThen\\n\\\\[\\nry\\\\equiv x^k\\\\pmod Q,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\((ry)^2\\\\equiv x^{2k}\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod Q\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nQ\\\\mid (ry)^2+1.\\n\\\\]\\nApplying the hypothesis with \\\\(n=r\\\\), and using \\\\(\\\\varphi(r)=r-1\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\nQ\\\\mid x^{r-1}-1.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_Q(x)=4k\\\\), we obtain \\\\(4k\\\\mid r-1\\\\), and in particular \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\mid r-1\\\\). But \\\\(r\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\), so \\\\(r-1\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod\\\\ell\\\\), a contradiction.\\n\\nThus \\\\(x>1\\\\) is impossible. Since \\\\(x\\\\) is a positive integer, necessarily \\\\(x=1\\\\).\"}",
